  "content": {
    "id": "p8-q12-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "12",
    "prompt": "Prove that $(\\sin\\theta+\\csc\\theta)^2+(\\cos\\theta+\\sec\\theta)^2-(\\tan\\theta+\\cot\\theta)^2=5$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$5$.\n\nExpand the squares. Use $\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta=1$, $\\sec^2\\theta-\\tan^2\\theta=1$ and $\\csc^2\\theta-\\cot^2\\theta=1$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(\\sin\\theta+\\csc\\theta)^2&=\\sin^2\\theta+2+\\csc^2\\theta\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}(\\cos\\theta+\\sec\\theta)^2&=\\cos^2\\theta+2+\\sec^2\\theta\\end{align*}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}-(\\tan\\theta+\\cot\\theta)^2&=-\\tan^2\\theta-2-\\cot^2\\theta\\end{align*}$$\nLet $L$ denote the left-hand side. Adding these expansions gives\n$$\\begin{align*}L&=(\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta)+(\\csc^2\\theta-\\cot^2\\theta)+(\\sec^2\\theta-\\tan^2\\theta)+2\\\\L&=1+1+1+2\\\\L&=5\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 62
  },
